 {
  "type": "post",
  "id": "7515522913815662592",
  "linkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/pentagram_mta-arts-design-has-been-bringing-art-to-activity-7515522913815662592-R9I4",
  "content": "MTA Arts & Design has been bringing art to New York’s transit system since 1985, through permanent station artworks, posters, and programs that put music and poetry in front of millions of riders a day. For the program’s 40th anniversary, Pentagram partner Giorgia Lupi and her team designed a commemorative identity for the yearlong celebration, alongside A Data Love Letter to the Subway, a data-driven animation commissioned for all 52 screens at Fulton Center.\n\nThe anniversary logo builds on the existing Arts & Design mark, two circles that join the MTA logo with the program’s name, adding a geometric, monolinear “40” that extends it into a single lockup. The team also introduced a bright, distinctive violet that gives the anniversary a tone of its own. In digital applications, the violet shifts into a subtle gradient toward a brighter lavender.\n\nThe campaign line, “Art Everywhere for Everyone,” sums up the program’s mission: art that lives throughout the system, free and open to every rider. The identity is built on a two-beat rhythm of call and response, from the paired circles to the “4” and “0” to “Everywhere” and “Everyone.” In motion, the line unfolds in two parts, and the logo animates in as the closing beat, its “40” writing on in a single stroke as the zero closes around “Since 1985.” This simple answering gesture, both celebratory and grounding, affirms Arts & Design’s longstanding promise to place and people.\n\nCheck out the full case study: https://lnkd.in/geUtMC5K",
